      "math_academy_id": "q-72770",
      "sequence_position": 23,
      "problem": "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=1$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by multiplying the $2$nd row by $4.$ \n\nSo, the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
